  "breakdowns": [],
  "method": "From each edition's table of estimated burdens of major taxes for a hypothetical family earning $75,000 (Table 1c in recent editions), the New York City row's total burden as a percent of income (income + property + sales + auto taxes). $75,000 is used because it is the income level the report uses for its headline maps of total, income, property and sales tax burdens, and it appears in every edition. Pages: 2008 edition PDF page 19 ($7,337 total); 2009 edition PDF page 19 ($8,173 total); 2010 edition PDF page 18 ($7,833 total); 2012 edition PDF page 18 ($8,692 total); 2013 edition PDF page 18 ($8,728 total); 2014 edition PDF page 25 ($8,293 total); 2015 edition PDF page 25 ($7,840 total); 2016 edition PDF page 26 ($8,871 total); 2017 edition PDF page 26 ($8,657 total); 2018 edition PDF page 26 ($8,785 total); 2019 edition PDF page 28 ($8,586 total); 2020 edition PDF page 28 ($8,621 total); 2021 edition PDF page 27 ($8,719 total); 2022 edition PDF page 27 ($7,494 total); 2023 edition PDF page 27 ($8,431 total).",
  "caveats": [
    "The report itself advises readers not to compare its hypothetical burdens across years, because small changes in its assumptions can make such comparisons misleading (2023 edition, PDF page 11); treat the series as a sequence of single-year comparisons across cities, not a trend.",
    "Editions cover taxes in effect through December 31 of the edition year; the latest edition found is 2023. No 2011 edition was found, so 2011 is missing.",
    "The report assumes the family owns its home at this income level (only the $25,000 family is assumed to rent), so the property tax figure depends on the report's housing-value assumption for each city."
  ]
